    "body": "<p>Bernie Krause, an American musician turned field recordist and soundscape ecologist, gave soundscape ecology its central vocabulary. He proposed that a natural soundscape has three distinct layers: biophony, the collective sound of living organisms; geophony, the non-biological sounds of the earth, including wind, water, rain, and thunder; and anthrophony, the sounds humans make, from speech and music to engines and industrial noise. Krause argued that healthy habitats sound organised, with species partitioning frequency and time so their voices do not mask one another, and that degraded habitats sound thinned or dominated by human noise. His book The Great Animal Orchestra, published in 2012, carried the argument to a wide audience, and his field recordings, some spanning decades at the same sites, are cited as evidence that soundscapes change measurably as habitats are altered. This record covers all three layers as distinct categories, and it is the suite&#x27;s authority for keeping them separate.</p><h4>Science proof</h4><p>Science proof: Krause&#x27;s acoustic niche hypothesis states that in an undisturbed habitat, vocal species evolve to occupy distinct frequency bands and time slots, reducing acoustic competition. Field comparisons support the broad pattern: intact habitats typically show sound spread across many frequency bands, while heavily disturbed sites show gaps where species have vanished and a concentration of low-frequency human noise. The hypothesis is a working model, not a law, and later research has refined it, but the three-layer vocabulary it introduced is now standard in soundscape ecology.</p><h4>Math proof</h4><p>Math proof: if a habitat&#x27;s audible band from 100 to 10000 hertz is divided into 10 equal bands of 990 hertz each, and 8 of the 10 bands hold biophony, the biophony band occupancy is 8 / 10 = 80.0 percent. The demo prints band width 990 Hz, occupied bands 8 of 10, and occupancy 80.0 percent, plus the anthrophony share if 2 bands are dominated by human noise, 2 / 10 = 20.0 percent.</p><h4>Soundscape layer(s)</h4><p>biophony; geophony; anthrophony</p><h4>Source</h4><p>Bernie Krause, The Great Animal Orchestra (2012) and The Power of Sound (earlier work on soundscape ecology); soundscape ecology literature on the acoustic niche hypothesis, as published October 2026</p>",
